<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	macOS Debug entitlements. The critical bit for Sprint-2-onward:
	`keychain-access-groups` is what gates kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain
	on macOS. Without it (and without a Distribution provisioning
	profile that grants application-identifier), every Keychain
	read/write returns OSStatus -34018 (errSecMissingEntitlement).
	$(AppIdentifierPrefix) is filled in by codesign from the team that
	signed the Debug build, mirroring the iOS Debug entitlements.

	Why the Debug app needs this and the legacy keychain doesn't help:
	macOS legacy keychain ACLs are tied to the signing identity. Apple
	Development re-signs the bundle on every Debug build with a fresh
	ad-hoc-ish certificate, so the previous launch's keychain items
	become unreadable to the new launch. Result: "every macOS launch
	needs a fresh invite." Data-protection keychain, in contrast, is
	bundle-id scoped — survives re-signs.

	No app-sandbox in Debug so dev-only flows (file pickers, debugger
	attach, etc.) stay frictionless. App-sandbox is only set in
	Release where the hardened runtime is also active.
	-->
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.nesttalk.macos</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
